ways for you to decide,

00:35:23.022 --> 00:35:27.740
when you have different options,
which one might you want to pursue.

00:35:27.740 --> 00:35:30.330
Remember, reflect, process, connect.

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:35.096
Process and connect could be flipped
here but I figured, remember, reflect,

00:35:35.096 --> 00:35:39.451
process, connect sounded a little
bit more like I would remember that.

00:35:39.451 --> 00:35:41.790
So, remember the things in your life.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:46.529
Remember the events, people, places,
achievements, things that you're proud of.

00:35:46.529 --> 00:35:49.290
Reflect on that circumstance, right?

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:52.407
Not just that event, but how did you feel?

00:35:52.407 --> 00:35:55.670
What were some of the situations and
circumstances surrounding it?

00:35:56.750 --> 00:36:02.447
That process and connect piece, how did
that inform anything else in your life?

00:36:02.447 --> 00:36:04.670
Are there connections here?

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:07.147
Because of that action, I learned.

00:36:07.147 --> 00:36:12.033
And then, hey, in this other instance,
I applied that learning.

00:36:12.033 --> 00:36:18.473
Or, hey, that was feedback,
now I'm seeing myself in new ways.

00:36:18.473 --> 00:36:23.120
Now this is changing the way I'm
thinking about approaching maybe this

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:25.064
field that I'm interested.

00:36:25.064 --> 00:36:28.653
And that all takes time,
be gentle with yourself.

00:36:28.653 --> 00:36:30.489
Take time for that introspection.

00:36:30.489 --> 00:36:33.779
Sometimes that introspection
can be done with others,

00:36:33.779 --> 00:36:35.880
it doesn't always have to be solo.

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:38.146
Find people that you can care about.

00:36:38.146 --> 00:36:43.026
Also think about who the mentors,
sponsors, and advocates in your life

00:36:43.026 --> 00:36:48.146
might be, so that they can help advise and
guide you through this process and

00:36:48.146 --> 00:36:53.592
give other valuable perspectives coming
from people who really care about you.

00:36:53.592 --> 00:36:56.195
And then, remember, it's yours.

00:36:56.195 --> 00:36:58.623
This belongs to you.

00:36:58.623 --> 00:37:02.060
I'm trying to get us to think
about that big picture, right?

00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:06.312
Think about that story as something
that you're able to adapt and

00:37:06.312 --> 00:37:08.407
flex to different situations.

00:37:08.407 --> 00:37:11.957
If you get stuck in a script and
we think about it as a script,

00:37:11.957 --> 00:37:15.310
coming off of that page
becomes really hard.

00:37:15.310 --> 00:37:19.899
But you are going to be in so
many interesting, fascinating and exciting

00:37:19.899 --> 00:37:25.133
spaces, not just as you apply to business
school, but during your time there.

00:37:25.133 --> 00:37:27.280
People are going to
want to learn about you.

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:31.639
Building these many relationships are
different ways that you can think about

00:37:31.639 --> 00:37:34.887
networking or
kind of exploring career options, right?

00:37:34.887 --> 00:37:39.600
So hopefully these tips and little tricks
can help you along the way as well.

00:37:40.940 --> 00:37:43.250
And remember,
you don't have to tell the whole story.

00:37:43.250 --> 00:37:46.060
Earlier I mentioned, some things,
we're still processing.

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:49.785
We're still trying to figure out
how that fits into the shape of me.

00:37:49.785 --> 00:37:51.240
All right, that's awesome.

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:52.462
Take that time.

00:37:52.462 --> 00:37:53.810
You own this.

00:37:53.810 --> 00:37:58.155
We want you to tell us
what you want us to hear.

00:37:58.155 --> 00:38:01.570
That's very different from,
what does Will want to to hear?

00:38:01.570 --> 00:38:03.623
What do I think Will wants to hear?

00:38:03.623 --> 00:38:07.163
Or what do I think Will wants
to read in an application, and

00:38:07.163 --> 00:38:09.200
shaping your narrative to that.

00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:12.371
Because you might lose
all of the different and

00:38:12.371 --> 00:38:17.329
beautiful aspects of yourself when
you're trying to conform to what you

00:38:17.329 --> 00:38:20.596
think might be someone
else's expectations.

00:38:20.596 --> 00:38:25.191
You are the one who is able to
give us that nuance and detail and

00:38:25.191 --> 00:38:28.160
insight into who you are.

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:32.327
I think this is a detail that I also
want to share, like, yes, there are a lot

00:38:32.327 --> 00:38:36.317
of people out there, things, that I say,
hey, this is what got me in.

00:38:36.317 --> 00:38:39.870
And for them, yes,
it might have helped them in this journey.

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:44.193
This is a holistic review process, so
we are looking at everything in context.

00:38:44.193 --> 00:38:48.886
So in actuality, that may or may not
have been the thing that caught them in.

00:38:48.886 --> 00:38:53.766
Even as we talk to our alumni and
our students, as awesome as they are,

00:38:53.766 --> 00:38:57.425
they don't know why they got in and
they never will.

00:38:57.425 --> 00:39:00.882
So, yes, be inspired,
kind of think through again,

00:39:00.882 --> 00:39:05.241
these are some of the people I could
be learning and growing with, and

00:39:05.241 --> 00:39:08.360
celebrate you, celebrate your journey.

00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:12.763
They will also be learning
from you in turn.

00:39:12.763 --> 00:39:16.191
No one can tell your
story the way you can.

00:39:16.191 --> 00:39:22.217
Use your words, your language, the way
that feels and fits like a good coat,

00:39:22.217 --> 00:39:27.687
like a good thing, so that when you
are submitting that application,

00:39:27.687 --> 00:39:32.247
you know that you are giving us
the fullness of who you are.

00:39:32.247 --> 00:39:37.880
So, with that, thank you,,
that was a lot of content.

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:39.809
I am going to turn to our Q&amp;A box now.

00:39:39.809 --> 00:39:42.410
I see that there's been a lot of action.

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:46.245
My colleagues have been answering
questions this whole time.

00:39:46.245 --> 00:39:48.531
But if you haven't done so already,

00:39:48.531 --> 00:39:51.793
I encourage you to start
MyStanford.mba account.

00:39:51.793 --> 00:39:53.409
With some quick questions,

00:39:53.409 --> 00:39:57.244
we're able to help collect events
that might be of interest to you,

00:39:57.244 --> 00:40:03.751
as well as Application tracker to see
just where are you in the process.

00:40:03.751 --> 00:40:06.791
Alright, let's stop share for a minute.

00:40:07.879 --> 00:40:10.159
And let's go to the Q&amp;A box.

00:40:13.028 --> 00:40:15.920
Alright, so there is a little
bit of process question here.

00:40:15.920 --> 00:40:18.740
So this is from Shannon there's,
it's been uploaded.

00:40:18.740 --> 00:40:22.073
So question about the review process,
would there be a group of admission

00:40:22.073 --> 00:40:24.829
officers reading the same application or
just one officer.

00:40:24.829 --> 00:40:28.471
I asked this because I believe different
people will weigh different values goals

00:40:28.471 --> 00:40:29.755
differently.

00:40:29.755 --> 00:40:34.015
For example, an admission officer who has
a mother may resonate with a candidate who

00:40:34.015 --> 00:40:36.955
talks about motherhood better
than a non mother officer.

00:40:38.095 --> 00:40:42.092
If possible, could you share how you
would read our stories to make sure that

00:40:42.092 --> 00:40:46.045
the personal difference in interpretation
would not bias your decision?

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:51.000
Thank you for this question.

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:55.653
I think this is one of those
things that in the admissions

00:40:55.653 --> 00:41:00.630
process we take very seriously a large,
like, bias.

00:41:00.630 --> 00:41:02.780
Yes, it's part of being human.

00:41:02.780 --> 00:41:07.320
It's one of those things that have
helped us evolutionarily over history.

00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:10.350
And, we're not in that fight for
survival anymore.

00:41:10.350 --> 00:41:14.129
And sometimes these biases, which may
have been helpful in other instances,

00:41:14.129 --> 00:41:18.470
may actually work against us in
the things that we're trying to achieve.

00:41:18.470 --> 00:41:23.327
And in the application review process,
this is one where that bias,

00:41:23.327 --> 00:41:29.390
you're right, it can play a difference
and, we do training around that.

00:41:29.390 --> 00:41:31.737
And so
there are going to be multiple readers for

00:41:31.737 --> 00:41:33.850
all the folks who are admitted, right?

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:38.340
We want to make sure that there are eyes
on kind of to ensure that we are doing as

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:41.719
fair and providing us fairs and
assessment as possible.

00:41:41.719 --> 00:41:45.685
During our trainings, we do these
every year, we talk about bias.

00:41:45.685 --> 00:41:48.979
What are some of the different things
that might influence the way you look at

00:41:48.979 --> 00:41:49.760
an application?

00:41:51.310 --> 00:41:54.170
Also it is an evidence based process.

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:58.621
As I was mentioning earlier, there
are elements where we're looking at past

00:41:58.621 --> 00:42:02.950
behavior as a great indicator for
how you might behave again in the future.

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:08.160
That is one of the things
that we're looking at.

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:12.044
When we're looking and saying, hey,
here is an instance, here's evidence.

00:42:12.044 --> 00:42:16.484
That those are the times that we're
able to make the strongest cases and

00:42:16.484 --> 00:42:20.184
we see hey, yeah,
this is why you should be here at the GSD.

00:42:20.184 --> 00:42:23.029
This is where that storytelling
element comes into play.

00:42:23.029 --> 00:42:27.422
And I say that not as kind of trying
to adapt the story to cater to

00:42:27.422 --> 00:42:29.122
the people in the room.

00:42:29.122 --> 00:42:33.964
But rather, we are thinking and
looking for diversity broadly defined

00:42:33.964 --> 00:42:38.821
as you are able to talk about who you are,
kind of to the fullest extent.

00:42:38.821 --> 00:42:43.735
That's when our readers were trained to
kind of recognize the biases that we have

00:42:43.735 --> 00:42:46.410
as humans and set that aside and
work with and

00:42:46.410 --> 00:42:48.886
through them to make sure we see, okay?

00:42:48.886 --> 00:42:51.370
Here's the journey for
this individual in context.

00:42:52.400 --> 00:42:54.890
Your journey might look very different
from someone else's journey.

00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:59.763
That doesn't mean yours is any more or
less difficult or appealing,

00:42:59.763 --> 00:43:02.130
it just means they're different.

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:06.260
And we want to make sure we're thinking
through how you've expressed your voice,

00:43:06.260 --> 00:43:09.210
talent and
made an impact in those settings.

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:11.260
So kind of to summarize that,

00:43:11.260 --> 00:43:15.900
we take bias very seriously in
our evaluation and process.

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:20.261
We do have multiple folks who are going to
be having eyes on your application as we

00:43:20.261 --> 00:43:22.180
move through the process.

00:43:22.180 --> 00:43:27.020
And we really care that we are providing
an evidence based recommendation.

00:43:27.020 --> 00:43:30.676
While we're kind of pulling together and
creating a diverse and

00:43:30.676 --> 00:43:33.599
dynamic class with from
all types of backgrounds.

00:43:35.610 --> 00:43:40.787
Okay, here's a question from Freddy,
about how you should split the essay,

00:43:40.787 --> 00:43:45.740
should be 77% personal story and
the 30% professional experience?

00:43:45.740 --> 00:43:47.651
Is there a general rule of thumb?

00:43:47.651 --> 00:43:50.729
And so we'll talk a little bit more
about this in tomorrow's session.

00:43:50.729 --> 00:43:55.199
But I think as you're trying to think
through kind of what elements of your

00:43:55.199 --> 00:43:59.610
story will now be important,
take a look at the question itself, okay?

00:44:01.330 --> 00:44:05.850
For some schools, there might be kind of
a recommendation for how you balance it.

00:44:05.850 --> 00:44:10.508
At Stanford, there's not, you'll notice
that our questions are pretty open ended.

00:44:10.508 --> 00:44:13.449
What matters the most to you and why?

00:44:13.449 --> 00:44:18.027
That does not have to be career or
profession related,

00:44:18.027 --> 00:44:21.920
we're really asking you
what matters to you.

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:26.502
And not just that, we're hoping that
you're doing some of the things we did

00:44:26.502 --> 00:44:29.224
together today and
really dig into the how and

00:44:29.224 --> 00:44:33.751
why to understand kind of the values and
perspective that you would bring.

00:44:33.751 --> 00:44:37.560
So there is no recommended
balance necessarily.

00:44:38.660 --> 00:44:42.131
You will notice that, there are questions
that are geared towards kind of that

00:44:42.131 --> 00:44:43.970
professional experience.

00:44:43.970 --> 00:44:47.160
So, the impact questions
that are optional.

00:44:47.160 --> 00:44:49.982
If you aren't already
discussing things there,

00:44:49.982 --> 00:44:54.619
that could be a place where you're talking
about work or kind of community or other

00:44:54.619 --> 00:44:59.840
types of impacts that you might have made
in the different spaces you would have it.

00:44:59.840 --> 00:45:02.990
So, each question might be
slightly different, but

00:45:02.990 --> 00:45:06.050
we are very intentional
about the way we word it.

00:45:06.050 --> 00:45:09.522
So we're not trying to fish for
some invisible proportion,

00:45:09.522 --> 00:45:14.550
we're really asking directly for
the things that we're looking for.

00:45:14.550 --> 00:45:18.980
So, hopefully that helps, and again,
there's no right or wrong balance.

00:45:18.980 --> 00:45:24.250
Everybody is going to kind of embrace and
engage with these questions differently.

00:45:24.250 --> 00:45:25.680
And I encourage you to do so, right?

00:45:25.680 --> 00:45:27.750
Do it in a way that feels right for you.

00:45:29.509 --> 00:45:31.520
Here's a question from Bluesky.

00:45:31.520 --> 00:45:34.530
Is Stanford more interested in
someone inspired to change the world?

00:45:34.530 --> 00:45:35.694
Or is it equally possible for

00:45:35.694 --> 00:45:38.760
someone who just wants to live a better
life through learning at Stanford?

00:45:40.210 --> 00:45:42.670
I think this is also one of those nuances.

00:45:42.670 --> 00:45:44.970
We talked a lot about change.

00:45:44.970 --> 00:45:48.008
How are you going to, like change lives,
change organizations,

00:45:48.008 --> 00:45:50.430
change the world, it's in our motto.

00:45:50.430 --> 00:45:53.110
I say, that's very aspiration, right?

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:57.462
We hope that you're able to dream big,
but that doesn't necessarily mean that we

00:45:57.462 --> 00:46:02.080
are looking specifically for folks who
are like, I need to go change the world.

00:46:02.080 --> 00:46:05.510
Sometimes you may just want to change
your little corner of it, right?

00:46:05.510 --> 00:46:07.960
Maybe it's those projects that
you're approaching at work.

00:46:07.960 --> 00:46:11.411
Maybe it is how you are moving
through your career and

00:46:11.411 --> 00:46:16.210
you want to be able to accelerate that and
change that trajectory.

00:46:16.210 --> 00:46:21.106
Those are all equally powerful,
relevant and valuable, like rationales for

00:46:21.106 --> 00:46:23.030
coming to Stanford.

00:46:23.030 --> 00:46:26.903
And so we don't have a preference for
whether you want to change the world, or

00:46:26.903 --> 00:46:30.910
you just want to change the way you kind
of see things and kind of shake things up.

00:46:30.910 --> 00:46:35.793
Your rationale is your own,
and I personally encourage you

00:46:35.793 --> 00:46:39.807
to celebrate and
really live into those, okay?

00:46:39.807 --> 00:46:41.909
And here's another question about,

00:46:41.909 --> 00:46:45.200
how would you advise on making
cliche topics interesting?

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:50.000
So, for example, wants to go to Stanford
because of the tight knit network.

00:46:51.310 --> 00:46:52.620
Center yourself.

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:55.760
We did a little bit of
that as an exercise.

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:59.006
But as you think about even
things like an answer,

00:46:59.006 --> 00:47:02.660
maybe family matters the most to you,
why, why again?

00:47:02.660 --> 00:47:07.270
What are some of the things that you as an
individual have experienced to shape that?

00:47:07.270 --> 00:47:09.295
I think that question of why is so
powerful and

00:47:09.295 --> 00:47:13.320
we'll play around with that a little
bit more in one of our other workshops.

00:47:13.320 --> 00:47:16.380
But really digging in and getting deeper.

00:47:16.380 --> 00:47:19.190
That's where that authenticity
piece comes to play.

00:47:19.190 --> 00:47:23.280
You're the only one who can talk about
your life and the nuances of it.

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:25.180
And so allow yourself to go there.

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:29.987
I think it really does surprise people,
how like encompassing the journey is and

00:47:29.987 --> 00:47:34.866
how much we're asking you to think about
things beyond just kind of that practical

00:47:34.866 --> 00:47:38.210
tactical, this is what I'm
going to do with my career.

00:47:38.210 --> 00:47:44.127
But also, here's who I am and this is how
that informs how I'm going to show up,

00:47:44.127 --> 00:47:46.690
here there in any other setting.

00:47:47.800 --> 00:47:51.670
So, I do want to be respectful
of everybody's time.

00:47:51.670 --> 00:47:52.920
Thank you so much.

00:47:52.920 --> 00:47:57.427
We will be going through these questions
and saving a number of them to ask Dimas

00:47:57.427 --> 00:48:00.590
this Friday,
[LAUGH] office hours with the Dimas.

00:48:00.590 --> 00:48:04.263
I think hearing from her is also
an amazing way to learn about kind of

00:48:04.263 --> 00:48:05.314
the philosophy and

00:48:05.314 --> 00:48:09.980
energy and the spirit of the GSB,
MBA application review process.

00:48:09.980 --> 00:48:13.791
So, thank you so much for
sharing in this afternoon, morning,

00:48:13.791 --> 00:48:18.950
evening whatever time of day it is,
thank you for spending this time with us.

00:48:18.950 --> 00:48:23.567
Hopefully you were able to take something
useful away, maybe it was a trick,

00:48:23.567 --> 00:48:27.190
activity, awareness, whatever it may be.

00:48:27.190 --> 00:48:29.299
I hope to cross paths with you again soon.

00:48:29.299 --> 00:48:33.099
Until then, thank you again, stay safe.

00:48:33.099 --> 00:48:37.433
And here is the creating your story.

00:48:37.433 --> 00:48:39.080
[MUSIC]

